<DocLayout
  title="Routers"
  description="Providers, presets, fallback, health, and routing strategy in Sarthi Studio."
>
  <p>
    A <strong>router</strong> sits between Studio and the model providers.
    It owns provider credentials, fallback chains, budgets, and the routing
    strategy — Studio never stores upstream provider keys.
  </p>

  <h2>Providers and presets</h2>
  <p>
    Settings ships presets for twelve OpenAI-compatible platforms: FreeLLMAPI,
    OmniRoute, Hugging Face, Groq, Cerebras, Gemini, OpenAI, OpenRouter,
    Mistral, Cloudflare, Together, and Novita. Pick a preset, paste the
    endpoint's API key into the router, and test the connection. With
    FreeLLMAPI, Studio can also manage the router's lifecycle (start and
    stop it on its own), and the embedded router console exposes providers,
    the model catalog, routing strategy, analytics, and logs over loopback.
  </p>

  <h2>Fallback and health</h2>
  <p>
    Fallback chains, circuit breaking, and provider health are owned by the
    router. They depend on available provider quota, so free tiers are not
    unlimited. Studio surfaces provider health and the usage/error log with
    latency and token counts when the provider reports them.
  </p>

  <h2>Routing strategy</h2>
  <p>
    The router decides which provider answers each request, in which order,
    and with what controls (Thinking, temperature, enforcement). Provider
    health is considered when a provider is degraded, and the fallback
    chain ensures the run keeps moving even when a provider is unreachable.
  </p>

  <h2>Local models</h2>
  <p>
    A local model served by Ollama needs no router key and no network: chat,
    context, and history all stay on the machine. Local models also serve
    embeddings for semantic index search when you prefix the embedding model
    with <code>local:</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Key handling</h2>
  <p>
    Provider keys are typed into the router, never stored by Studio. Studio
    keeps only the router's unified key, server-side, in a
    permission-restricted configuration file. This keeps the model traffic
    and your credentials inside your machine.
  </p>

  <h2>Honest limits</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Free-tier quotas are not unlimited; fallbacks depend on provider health.</li>
    <li>Price and context-window figures are shown only when a provider publishes them.</li>
    <li>A router you launch yourself is not stopped by Studio on exit.</li>
  </ul>
</DocLayout>
